  "why": "I read the statement as asking for the values of the classical two-colour numbers R(r,s), with R(5,5) singled out. An event that moves the bounds on a specific value is therefore a `bound`: Angeltveit-McKay's R(5,5)<=46, AlphaEvolve's lower bounds for R(3,13), R(3,18) and R(4,13)-R(4,20), Coniglio et al.'s R(3,n) lower bounds, and, before the snapshot, R(3,10)<=41, Exoo and the earlier R(5,5) upper bounds. Results about how fast these numbers grow (the CGMS optimisation, the R(3,k) constants, Ma-Shen-Xie, Bradac, OpenAI 170) never fix or bound any individual value, so they answer a different question: `variant`. Multicolour, cycle-clique, hypercube and doubly saturated graph results are `variant` for the same reason. Formalisations, the heuristic claim, the conditional structural paper and the partial SAT exclusion are `none`. Molnar's search is `instances`. AlphaEvolve gets `generated` because the evolved search programs produced the witness graphs. Steiner's v2 is listed as withdrawn on arXiv, so I treated the event as `knocked_down`. The attributions of Bradac's exponent and the triangle result to OpenAI rest only on third parties and are not the authors' own account, so both are `none`.",
